  "enhanced_information": "For a 71-year-old patient with severe aplastic anemia, the primary treatment options include immunosuppressive therapy with agents like cyclosporine and antithymocyte globulin, especially if no HLA-matched sibling donor is available for allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation. Supportive care is crucial to manage the risks of infection and bleeding associated with pancytopenia. High-dose steroids and chemotherapy with autologous transplant are not standard treatments for this condition and are generally not recommended.",
  "analysis": "The fundamental therapeutic approach for severe aplastic anemia involves either immunosuppressive therapy or hematopoietic stem cell transplantation, depending on the availability of a suitable donor and the patient's condition. The retrieved medical knowledge supports the use of cyclosporine therapy for treating severe aplastic anemia, which aligns with the standard treatment protocol of immunosuppressive therapy using cyclosporine and antithymocyte globulin (ATG). This is a well-established treatment for patients who are not suitable candidates for stem cell transplantation or do not have an available HLA-matched donor. Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation is indeed a curative treatment for severe aplastic anemia, particularly in younger patients with a matched sibling donor, but the initial approach in many cases, especially in older patients or those without a donor, is immunosuppressive therapy.",
